What remains undone

T says the cancelled-run retry is repaired by #558 and #560. At M, approve_held (warrant/gate.py:1519-1524) still builds `live` from ANY earlier run at the same head whose conclusion is not in ENDED_WITHOUT_CHECK. An old successful run therefore marks the workflow live even when its newer run completed as cancelled. check_reasons (warrant/gate.py:589-610) instead reads the latest required check by id and returns CHECK_RED for that cancelled run. The gate neither merges the exact revert nor dispatches its required workflow again. enforce_brakes (warrant/gate.py:1572-1593) also skips it as pending. Every subsequent run with those same inputs remains stuck; the timely hold-label veto is not carried through to a revert merge.

Counterexample and controls

The probe uses the current repository's signed fixture store and real Git trees, creates a braked merge of AGENTS.md and its exact revert on the gate's own branch, and runs the actual assess, enforce_brakes and approve_held functions. The stub returns the hold label for the original merged PR. The required workflow has two workflow_dispatch runs at the same revert head: id 1 success and id 2 cancelled, returned newest first. Matching check runs have the same order and suite bindings. This is synthetic API data, not a claim that this incident occurred on GitHub.

Observed: exact_revert=true, codes=[CHECK_RED], enforce_brakes=[], retry=[], dispatch=[].
Expected: the cancelled latest required check must be recoverable automatically despite the older success. Align retry selection with the check/run that currently blocks merging, or consistently permit reuse of the prior success; it must not remain CHECK_RED while a historical success suppresses recovery. Keep the failed-run guard and current-head checks.

Controls: success alone is eligible and dispatches nothing; cancelled alone is CHECK_RED and dispatches ci.yml once. Thus the reproducer isolates the historical-success suppression and is not a test that demands re-running a failed suite.

